# Auth Security Checklist

Never share API keys in prompts

Don't include your actual Supabase URL or anon key in prompts
Use placeholders like "YOUR_SUPABASE_URL" and replace them manually later
Store sensitive keys in environment variables or a secure config file


Review generated code carefully

Always review authentication code generated by the AI
Pay special attention to how user credentials are handled
Ensure passwords are never stored in plain text or logged


Avoid sharing personal/user data

Don't share real user data in your prompts
Use dummy/sample data for testing and demonstrations
Anonymize any data you need to share with the AI


Check permissions in generated code

Ensure Row Level Security (RLS) policies are properly implemented
Verify that data access is properly restricted to authenticated users


Validate input handling

Make sure the generated code properly validates user input
Check for proper error handling around authentication attempts


Examine token management

Ensure tokens are stored securely (not in plain localStorage)
Verify proper token refresh mechanisms are in place


Be cautious with generated SQL

Review any SQL statements for potential injection vulnerabilities
Ensure parametrized queries are used consistently


Inspect password reset flows

Verify that password reset mechanisms are secure
Check that verification emails work as expected
